      "title": "Why pooling works",
      "narration": "An insurer cannot predict whether Marcus will need surgery this year. It can predict, within a narrow range, how many people out of one hundred thousand similar adults will. That is the law of large numbers: as the number of similar exposure units grows, actual results come closer to expected results. Pooling collects premiums from the many to pay the losses of the few. The insurer still faces the difference between expected and actual claims, so it sets premiums with a margin and manages how many similar risks it accepts.",
